steps.prompt_template:
The user asked a question about this workspace. Find out and answer it, using your read tools. If they asked you to do one thing the app does with a button (run a workflow or a schedule now, turn a schedule on or off, answer or approve what waits, allow or refuse a tool call, stop a run), put the card up with `prepare_action` and say what pressing it does; never say it is done.

What they asked:
{state.user_message}

Conversation so far:
{state.chat_history}

Workspace profile (what this organisation does):
{state.org_profile}

A plan, if one was drafted before it turned out to change nothing (empty otherwise) — carry out its read-only steps rather than starting over:
{state.manage_plan}

What this product's own help, its Connect card or its list of what it can and cannot do returned for this question (empty otherwise). Answer from it: name the screens it names, say a Connect card is above when one was put up, and say plainly what the product cannot do with the nearest thing that works. Never describe a screen it does not name:
{state.looked_up}

If the context has `current_page`, that is what the user has open on screen — 'this' and 'it' mean it. If `current_orchestration_source` is `ambiguous`, or they asked to change an orchestration and `current_orchestration_id` is empty, do not guess and do not change anything. When `current_orchestration_matches` lists the candidates, show them as a numbered list in exactly that order and ask them to reply with the number or the full name — their reply is matched against that list. Otherwise say which orchestrations exist (from `list_orchestrations`) and ask for the full name, or to open it and ask from there.

The person wants something done that this workspace has no app or tool for yet. Find out who can do it and how it would connect here, and report the options.

What they asked:
{state.user_message}

Conversation so far:
{state.chat_history}

Workspace profile (what this organisation does, where):
{state.org_profile}

The user approved this plan. Carry it out exactly — no more, no less.

Approved plan:
{state.manage_plan}

What the user said when approving (may contain a last-minute detail):
{state.manage_review}

Their original request, for context:
{state.user_message}

Report what you actually did, one line per operation, including anything that failed and why.
